  <data name="RemoteDialogTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Remote control</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteDialogIntroFormat" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Turn this on, and the sequencer makes its own MIDI endpoint, {0}. MIDI Glass, another app, or a controller routed through MIDI Patchbay can send to it to launch clips and scenes, run the transport, and mute, solo or arm tracks. The sequencer sends back what's playing, so a surface can light up.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteSwitchHeader" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Remote control endpoint</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteMapHeading" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Mappings</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteMapTracks" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Channels 1 to 15 are tracks 1 to 15, in the order of the track list, with folders left out. On a track's channel, notes 0 to 63 launch the clips in scenes 1 to 64, note 64 stops the track, and note 65 sends it back to the timeline. Controllers 20, 21 and 22 mute, solo and arm it.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteMapSequence" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Channel 16 is the whole sequence. Notes 0 to 63 launch scenes 1 to 64, note 64 stops every track, 65 sends every track back to the timeline, 96 plays, 97 stops, 98 records, 99 turns the loop on or off, 100 turns the metronome on or off, and 101 goes back to the start.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteMapMore" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Tracks 16 and up are on group 2 and up. A MIDI 1.0 controller can send all of these. Keep remote control on in only one sequencer window at a time: other apps find the endpoint by its name.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteMakeLayout" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Create MIDI Glass layout…</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemoteMakeLayoutHelp" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A layout with a pad for each clip, a button for each scene, the transport, and mute, solo and arm for each track. It opens in MIDI Glass and turns remote control on. After you add tracks or scenes, make it again: a new file leaves the old one alone.</value>
  </data>
